---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import DevEncodeChmod from '@/components/tools/dev/DevEncodeChmod.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="chmod-calculator">
  <DevEncodeChmod client:load />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>Three classes, three permissions</h2>
    <p>
      Unix permissions answer nine yes/no questions: may the owner, the group and everyone else read,
      write and execute? Each permission has a value (read 4, write 2, execute 1), and each class gets
      the sum of its permissions as one octal digit.
    </p>
    <pre><code>rwx = 4 + 2 + 1 = 7
rw- = 4 + 2     = 6
r-x = 4     + 1 = 5
r-- = 4         = 4</code></pre>
    <p>Three digits, one per class, give the familiar 644, 755 or 600.</p>

    <h2>What the permissions mean on a directory</h2>
    <p>
      On a directory, the same three letters mean something quite different:
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Bit</th><th>On a file</th><th>On a directory</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>read (r)</td><td>Open and read the contents</td><td>List the names inside, but not read them</td></tr>
          <tr><td>write (w)</td><td>Modify or truncate the file</td><td>Create, rename and delete entries inside</td></tr>
          <tr><td>execute (x)</td><td>Run it as a program</td><td>Traverse it, to reach anything inside</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      This has two results. First, a directory needs execute to be useful: with read alone you see
      names but can open nothing. Second, deleting a file depends on the write permission of its
      directory, not of the file. You can delete a read-only file that you do not own if you can write
      to its folder. The sticky bit closes that gap.
    </p>

    <h2>The special bits</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Bit</th><th>Octal</th><th>On an executable</th><th>On a directory</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>setuid</td><td>4000</td><td>Runs as the file's owner, whoever starts it</td><td>No effect on Linux</td></tr>
          <tr><td>setgid</td><td>2000</td><td>Runs as the file's group</td><td>New entries inherit the directory's group</td></tr>
          <tr><td>sticky</td><td>1000</td><td>Historic, now ignored</td><td>Only the owner of an entry may delete it</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      In <code>ls -l</code> these replace the execute character: <code>s</code> where execute is also
      set, capital <code>S</code> where it is not, and <code>t</code> or <code>T</code> in the same way
      for sticky. A capital <code>S</code> usually shows a mistake: a setuid bit on a file that nobody
      can execute.
    </p>

    <h2>Why 777 keeps appearing, and why it is wrong</h2>
    <p>
      777 is the fastest way to make a permission error go away, so forum answers often suggest it. It
      lets each account on the machine, including any compromised service, rewrite the file. On a web
      root, this turns a minor vulnerability into remote code execution.
    </p>
    <p>
      The correct fix is almost always ownership. Set the group of the file to the group the web server
      runs as, then use 664 for files and 775 for directories. If you cannot change ownership, fix the
      deployment; do not work around it.
    </p>

    <h2>Modes you will actually use</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Mode</th><th>Symbolic</th><th>For</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>644</td><td>rw-r--r--</td><td>Ordinary files: documents, HTML, images</td></tr>
          <tr><td>755</td><td>rwxr-xr-x</td><td>Scripts, binaries and directories</td></tr>
          <tr><td>600</td><td>rw-------</td><td>SSH private keys, .env files; SSH refuses looser modes</td></tr>
          <tr><td>700</td><td>rwx------</td><td>~/.ssh and other private directories</td></tr>
          <tr><td>664 / 775</td><td>rw-rw-r-- / rwxrwxr-x</td><td>Shared group editing</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1777</td><td>rwxrwxrwt</td><td>/tmp: everyone writes, only owners delete</td></tr>
          <tr><td>2775</td><td>rwxrwsr-x</td><td>A project directory where new files keep the team group</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Symbolic notation</h2>
    <p>
      <code>chmod u+x script.sh</code> adds execute for the owner and changes nothing else.
      <code>chmod u=rwx,go=rx script.sh</code> sets each class exactly and clears all permissions not
      listed. This page generates the equals form, because it gives the same result from any starting
      mode, which is what a script or a runbook needs.
    </p>
    <p>
      A warning about recursion: <code>chmod -R 755</code> makes all files executable, including
      documents and images. Use <code>chmod -R u=rwX,go=rX</code>: the capital <code>X</code> sets
      execute only on directories and on files that already had it.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
